\details{
\strong{Relative activity.} The denominator is the sum over all external
promoters of the gene, fixed before any promoter is filtered, so a
promoter dropped by the filter still counts in its gene's denominator.
Internal promoters are left out: proActiv does not quantify them. Where
the gene's sum is 0 the relative activity is \code{NA}.

\strong{Filter.} In this order (each step reported in \code{steps}):
\enumerate{
\item \code{require_active}: a promoter must be active in at least one
condition. For a \code{from_proactiv()} result these are proActiv's classes:
not \code{"Inactive"} in at least one condition, and classified in every
condition (as in the paper). For a matrix, active means a mean
absolute activity of at least \code{min_abs} in at least one group of
\code{group}, proActiv's rule.
\item Promoters of genes with one annotated promoter (internal promoters
included) are dropped.
\item In at least one group (not necessarily the same one for each
criterion), a promoter must have a share of samples with a relative
activity above 0 greater than \code{min_nonzero}, a share of missing values
less than \code{max_missing}, and a mean relative activity (over the samples
with a value) greater than \code{min_mean}. The shares are over all samples
of the group.
\item Genes left with fewer than \code{min_promoters} promoters are dropped.
}
As in the paper, the criteria of step 3 are evaluated on proActiv's own
relative activity when \code{x} comes from \code{\link[=from_proactiv]{from_proactiv()}} (its denominator
also holds the internal promoters), and on the relative activity computed
here when \code{x} is a matrix. The values returned are always the ones
computed here. Steps 2 and 4 together keep genes with at least two kept
promoters; step 2 only changes the counts reported in between.

The defaults are the paper's values. With them, on the paper's proActiv
results (GTEx v8 RNA-seq, 22,865 samples, 57 tissues as \code{group}), the
92,557 external promoters of 52,037 genes come down to 10,826 promoters in
4,744 genes.
